00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:09.490 align:start position:0%
a caravan of men and Giants crosses the  land they reach a moment of decision  allow a troop of thieves to join the  roving party in the hopes they might  bolster the defenses or send them away  in fear assuming they will steal  precious supplies in the Banner Saga you  face these kinds of decisions all  through the game's ten-hour campaign  making choices and then clenching your  jaw and hoping your trust was properly  placed sometimes it is and sometimes it  isn't making decisions with unclear  consequences is the banner sagas main  thrust you spend most of your time  watching a convoy of Men and Giants  cross the frigid Scandinavian landscape  and rest in the villages and encampments  they encounter the automated journey is  interrupted frequently with events that  require your input a woman has been  murdered and her mother blames another  traveler do you support the accuser do  you support the accused or do you dig  deeper into the conflict  you can't presume anything kindness can  lead to disaster harshness can lead to  triumph all you can do is follow your  instincts for the best this is an  intriguing approach to strategy and  storytelling and it often works your  convoy is made up of Klansmen members of  the giant like Varro race and human  warriors each day spent hiking uses up  supplies should you run out you start  losing followers to famine furthermore  the same resource you use to purchase  more supplies in the towns you visit is  the same resource you use to level up  your heroes who you lead him to  turn-based battle the story takes an  impressive number of turns based on your  choices weaving a twisted tapestry of  unlikely alliances and disastrous  betrayals choices might manifest  immediately or quite sometime later but  the Banner Saga never goes out of its  way to point out its own intricacies it  allows events to simply occur and as a  result the decision making process feels  ganic rather than gamy and contrived  that said there are times when I wish  there were a more obvious and consistent  connection between my decisions and the  results and by the time it had drawn to  a close the story I had co-authored had  gone off the rails a major plot point  had been almost entirely buried and  while I was moved by the ending I was  shocked when the credits rolled  immediately afterwards the main enemy  forces are made up of a marauding evil  known as the dredge battles occur on a  grid and if you've ever played a  turn-based strategy game you'll be  immediately familiar with the basics  even before combat begins you must  consider which heroes to bring to battle  choose wisely different heroes have some  different special abilities and if they  haven't recovered from the previous  battle they're less effective in this  one on the battlefield you must pay  attention to armor values as well as  health values as well as maximizing the  amount of damage you can do with any  given hero moment-to-moment  the Banner Saga skirmishes are much like  those in similar battle systems but a  rock-solid interface of fantastic  animated art style and varied abilities  keep the combat compelling though it's  rarely overtly challenging until you  near the game's final hour even more  compelling is the banner sagas  atmosphere - colourful hand-drawn  characters and cel-shaded villages would  have been at home in a high budget  animated film and the site of your  Caravan stretching across the screen  with the yellow banner flying from an ox  card on the horn viral trudging wearily  onward hammers home the viciousness of  the elements Banner Saga is a beautiful  game filled with interesting ideas and  enjoyable battles it's too bad it trips  over itself so clumsily by the end but  this chilly journey does a fine job of  reminding you that being a respectable  leader is about more than just giving  rousing speeches and making alliances  [Music]
 

